00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:15.280
In the past four decades, natural disasters
have killed 60,000 people, on average, a year.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:21.449
This includes things like earthquakes, droughts,
epidemics, and fires, although, of course,

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:24.449
not all disasters are equally memorable.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:28.850
Researchers have found that to get the same
amount of media attention, a famine must kill

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:36.040
38,000 people, while a drought only has to
kill 2,400, an epidemic 1,600, and a volcano

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.760
eruption, just 1.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.900
To catch the most eyes, a catastrophe should
be shocking, scary, ongoing, and, most of

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.700
all, uncertain.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:53.239
The Coronavirus, or COVID-19, as it’s officially
known, ticks all these boxes, and then some:

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:59.180
the threat is invisible to the naked eye,
deadly, foreign, and changing every day.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:05.119
Here’s what we know: Although the number
has since grown, at 120,000 confirmed cases,

00:01:05.119 --> 00:01:12.799
there were about 4,300 deaths, giving it an
estimated fatality rate of 3.6%, significantly

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:18.149
lower than SARs, at about 9.5%, or MERs, at
34%.

00:01:18.149 --> 00:01:23.659
Although, we also know this number highly
depends on where and who you are.

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:29.240
Cardiovascular disease, high blood sugar,
and chronic respiratory disease, for example,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.579
greatly increase your chance of death.

00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:34.270
Even more significant is your age.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:41.009
The fatality rate drops to just 0.1% for 10-19
year-olds, and remains less than half a percentage

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:43.729
point for anyone under 50.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:46.979
After that, the odds rapidly deteriorate.

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:57.179
From 1.3% for those in their 50s, to 3.6%
for 60s, 8% for 70s, and 14.8% for anyone

00:01:57.179 --> 00:01:58.179
older.

00:01:58.179 --> 00:02:03.859
The vast majority, about 80% of those infected,
experience mild to moderate symptoms - most

00:02:03.859 --> 00:02:10.149
commonly fever, dry cough, and/or exhaustion
- and then recover, many, on their own and

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:12.290
some without even knowing.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:17.400
And because these symptoms are so common,
the most minor of cases likely aren’t reported

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.459
to authorities, which may make the fatality
rate appear far worse than it truly is.

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:28.400
That, in fact, is also the biggest challenge:
A low fatality rate for the young and healthy

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.510
makes it very easy to spread, which comes
at the expense of more vulnerable groups.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:38.440
The incubation period is estimated anywhere
from 1 to 14 days.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.969
If this is true, those infected may spread
the virus, undetected, most likely by coughs

00:02:43.969 --> 00:02:49.060
and sneezes, although it can also be found
in blood, stool, and tears, for one or even

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.200
two weeks without knowing they have it.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.879
Scarier than everything we know combined,
though, is everything we don’t.

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:02.939
It’s not yet understood where it originated,
who first contracted it, which animal transmitted

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:06.150
it, or even how, exactly, it began.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:10.930
Although the first cases were initially linked
to a Wuhan seafood market, later analysis

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.659
has cast doubt on this theory.

00:03:13.659 --> 00:03:19.129
These large gaps in our understanding leave
considerable room for anyone to fill as they

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:20.129
wish.

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:25.579
Thus, the wide range of reactions: Depending
on where you look, you will either see warnings

00:03:25.579 --> 00:03:30.819
that the situation is far worse than reported
and may eventually kill hundreds of thousands

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.989
or pleas to remain calm and comparisons to
the seasonal flu.

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:41.749
Until recently, about 80% of all confirmed
cases were located in Mainland, China,

00:03:41.749 --> 00:03:47.959
Meaning about 80% of what we know is based
on information provided by that one source.

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:52.840
Which version someone is inclined to believe,
therefore, is largely a litmus test of how

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.629
much he or she trusts China.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:04:00.930
There’s little doubt that once its leadership
was committed, China acted incredibly quickly,

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:04.670
making decisions that would be layered with
bureaucracy elsewhere.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:10.049
Famously, China seized the spotlight as an
opportunity to demonstrate its political effectiveness

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:15.969
on the world stage, swiftly converting universities,
convention centers, and schools into makeshift

00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:17.289
hospitals.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:22.390
According to a New York Times estimate, over
700 million Chinese have been simultaneously

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:28.580
quarantined - a feat that requires an unthinkable
degree of both political and cultural solidarity.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:33.990
It’s state-owned telecoms collected travel
records of every phone in the country, enabling

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:38.349
checkpoints to quickly deny access to high-risk
individuals.

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:43.650
In that same spirit of national brand-building,
Chinese diplomats have given over 400 media

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:49.979
interviews, published over 300 articles, and
the city of Shanghai sent 500,000 face masks

00:04:49.979 --> 00:04:52.710
to the center of the South Korean outbreak.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.470
China has tried to disassociate itself from
the outbreak, suggesting it may have originated

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:58.470
elsewhere.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:03.560
It’s also repeatedly congratulated itself
for slowing its spread, which it argues would

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.270
be far worse if not for its diligent work.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:11.760
What can’t be overlooked, however, is the
phrase “Once its leadership was committed”.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.949
Too often journalists fall into the trap of
either only celebrating China’s operational

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:21.090
efficiency or fixating on its authoritarian
pitfalls.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:27.810
Clearly, the same top-down system responsible
for building a hospital in 10 days also incentivized

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:33.740
local government officials to suppress crucial
early information about the virus and prioritize

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:37.319
party loyalty above immediate human suffering.

00:05:37.319 --> 00:05:42.400
Its genuinely-impressive successes exist in
the context of failures like the example of

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.820
Wuhan-based doctor Li Wenliang.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:51.800
On December 30th, over a week before the first
confirmed death, Li sent a message in a private

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.979
doctors’ WeChat group warning of a new SARs-like
outbreak.

00:05:55.979 --> 00:06:01.380
Just hours later, 8 doctors from the group,
including Li, were arrested by Wuhan Police

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:06.190
for spreading rumors and disrupting social
order, as announced by the Chinese National

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:07.680
Health Commission.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.680
After being compelled to sign a document admitting
his alleged crimes, Li heroically returned

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:18.159
to work, where he would contract and die from
the very virus he tried to stop.

00:06:18.159 --> 00:06:23.990
While his story is particularly tragic, it’s
only made exceptional by his personal sacrifice.

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:28.180
The forces which tried to stop him are sadly,
not.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:34.259
For every rare example of bravery, there are
undoubtedly countless more scared into silence.

00:06:34.259 --> 00:06:39.229
And if even doctors must be this devoted to
the party, there’s only more reason to doubt

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:41.430
government-compiled data.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:47.490
If numbers were misreported, outright fabricated,
or even just poorly or hastily collected,

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:52.169
they could drastically skew global measures
like fatality and infection rate.

00:06:52.169 --> 00:06:56.970
Thus, the large discrepancy of these numbers
between countries.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:05.400
On March 6th, the U.S. had a reported fatality
rate of 5.9%, while South Korea reported 0.6.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.090
What these numbers obscure, are the number
of people tested.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:15.439
America’s number, for example, was based
on a pathetic then-239 identified cases and

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:21.439
14 deaths, to Korea’s then-6,593 and 42.

00:07:21.439 --> 00:07:25.960
The lesson of this chart is that the easiest
way to maintain a low infection rate is simply

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:32.310
not to test for it - something the U.S. has
thus far excelled at - up until recently making

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:37.849
test kits inaccessible to anyone not exposed
to a confirmed case or having recently traveled

00:07:37.849 --> 00:07:39.510
to an infected region.

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:45.139
South Korea, on the other hand, had tested
8,000 people prior to its first case on January

00:07:45.139 --> 00:07:46.139
20th.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:50.719
The next week, it would go on to test 82,000
people across the country.

00:07:50.719 --> 00:07:56.030
By making this process easy, like with drive-through
stations, it was able to screen about 10,000

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:58.030
people every single day.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:02.949
The most dangerous kind of misinformation,
therefore, aren’t crazy claims about fake

00:08:02.949 --> 00:08:09.219
cures, which are easily exposed and dispelled,
but the treating of reported data as infallible,

00:08:09.219 --> 00:08:15.410
which creates unnecessary alarm in some places
and a dangerous sense of comfort in others.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:21.260
In truth, everything except the very basics
should be viewed with some degree of skepticism.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:25.349
By the time you’re watching this video,
the situation may have changed, and many of

00:08:25.349 --> 00:08:28.030
these numbers may no longer be accurate.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.810
At the same time, it’s important not to
panic.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:35.970
While it’s true that so much is unknown,
the precautions are simple and straightforward.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:41.420
This is the paradox of the Coronavirus: Although
the medical solutions are simple - the World

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:46.250
Health Organization recommends washing your
hands with soap and water or an alcohol-based

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:51.720
sanitizer, regularly, and not touching your
face - stopping the virus is not easy.

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:57.360
In practice, no government on Earth can effectively
manage and guarantee the compliance of every

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.590
one of its citizens - especially in democracies
where government power is limited.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:07.390
While the virus appears to be waning domestically,
in China, it’s entered a distinct second

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:15.120
phase of international spread, to Italy, Iran,
France, Germany, Spain, the U.S., and elsewhere.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:20.450
In the absence of the tight control made impossible
at this scale, which countries succeed in

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:24.850
containing their outbreaks largely depends
on their level of public trust.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:30.390
A good point of comparison are the cases of
Hong Kong and Singapore - two places with

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:35.140
similar population sizes, climates, and international
exposure.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:40.170
At first glance, Hong Kong would seem to be
at far greater risk, with 13 border crossings

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:45.350
to mainland China while Singapore is three
and a half thousand kilometers away.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:50.240
Even today, in response to the virus, three
crossings remain open in Hong Kong, while

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:55.790
Singapore became one of the first countries
to deny entry to all PRC-passport holders

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.730
and anyone who’s been to the mainland in
the past 14 days.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:05.000
Its government was quick to distribute masks
- 4 for every household, whereas Hong Kong

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:10.800
faced shortages, during which long lines formed
outside pharmacies for hours, as customers

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.690
waited for new shipments.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:17.890
In Singapore, self-employed quarantined residents
and holders of work passes are given about

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:23.680
70 U.S. dollars a day, along with free medical
treatment, to incentivize good behavior.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:29.050
Those who break quarantine laws are fined
up to about $7,200 USD and face as much as

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:31.380
six months of jail time.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:36.030
Authorities in Hong Kong, on the other hand,
began the crisis with little to no public

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:40.980
trust, and only lost more after its slow closing
of the border and failure to procure masks.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:42.520
Its quarantine-breaking punishments were weak,
resulting in several confirmed breaches.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:47.850
Worse, this often well-deserved distrust spirals
into a self-propelling panic.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:51.780
Because Hong Kongers didn’t believe their
government could or would protect them, the

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:56.310
most panicked began hoarding supplies, fearing
they might run out.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.690
Everyone else followed suit in a dangerous
game of musical chairs.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:05.250
And when the music stops, it’s most often
vulnerable groups, like doctors, the disabled,

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:06.970
and the poor who lose.

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:11.800
It’s also worth noting however, that Hong
Kong’s wide-spread distrust predisposed

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:16.700
its residents to take matters into their own
hands, creating a more vigilant public.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:21.820
A more severe example is Iran, where extreme
suspicion of government has resulted in an

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:26.570
outright crisis, inescapable by even the highest
levels of authority.

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:33.200
This pattern has repeated time and time, and
time, again, anywhere trust is low.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:39.770
The lesson, repeated but never absorbed, with
SARs, Ebola, MERs, and now today, is that,

00:11:39.770 --> 00:11:44.580
while the problem is a medical one for scientists
to research, and develop treatments for, the

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.850
suffering is most often self-inflicted.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:53.600
The actions of people and governments make
the difference between calm and chaos.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:58.120
After the virus is contained, its treatment
or vaccine, hopefully, discovered, and the

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:03.590
media moves on to more shocking stories, we
must not forget how much was preventable.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:08.870
Otherwise, the same forces and incentives
which aggravated the problem will only remain

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:15.700
dormant - waiting for the next outbreak, and
enabled by our disinterest.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:21.420
